<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan Palach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan Palach was a Czech student who set himself on fire in Wenceslas Square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 16 January 1969 in protest against the public's resignation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion. He died three days later, and his funeral became a mass demonstration. Commemorations in January 1989, known as Palach Week, were among the protests that preceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jan-palach.jpg" alt="Jan Palach" width="158" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Úřady · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Palach_foto_z_pr%C5%AFkazu.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jan Palach was born on 11 August 1948 and attended elementary school in Všetaty, where he was an avid reader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-verne">Jules Verne</a>. His father died when he was 13, and he was raised in part by his grandfather. He graduated from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> in Mělník in 1966, attended the Prague School of Economics, and in September 1968 transferred to the Faculty of Arts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">Charles University</a>, where he studied history and political economics. In the summer of 1967 he worked on a chicken farm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, where he instigated a strike with other Czech students that reduced their working day from ten hours to eight. On 7 November 1968 he took part in a demonstration for Czech independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 16 January 1969, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion that crushed the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>'s government, Palach set himself on fire in Wenceslas Square and suffered burns over 85 percent of his body. In a letter to several public figures he demanded the abolition of censorship and a halt to the distribution of Zprávy, the newspaper of the Soviet occupying forces, and called for a general strike. The burns specialist Jaroslava Moserová, who first treated him, said that he acted against the demoralisation of Czechoslovak citizens that the occupation had caused. He died in hospital three days later.</p><p>His funeral became a major protest against the occupation. On 25 February another student, Jan Zajíc, burned himself to death at the same place, and Evžen Plocek did so at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihlava">Jihlava</a> in April. Palach was first buried at the Olšany Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. As the grave became a national shrine the secret police exhumed his remains on 25 October 1973 and cremated them; his ashes were returned to the Prague grave on 25 October 1990.</p><p>Between 15 and 21 January 1989, protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> ostensibly in his memory but intended as anticommunist demonstrations grew into Palach Week, which the police suppressed. It is considered one of the demonstrations that preceded the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> ten months later. A bronze cross marks the spot outside the National Museum where he fell, and Jan Palach Square in Prague is named for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jan Palach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Prague Spring of 1968, Alexander Dubček's attempt at 'socialism with a human face', and its suppression shaped reform communism and Eurocommunism across Europe, and <b>Jan Palach</b>'s self-immolation in January 1969 became an emblem of protest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On 16 January 1969 the student <b>Jan Palach</b> set himself on fire in Wenceslas Square to protest against the renewed suppression of free speech.</blockquote><a href="/movement/prague-spring">Prague Spring · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Borůvkovo sanatorium, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.08,14.43&amp;year=1961" aria-label="Borůvkovo sanatorium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Jan Palach"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5784 5758h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5784 5758h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.08,14.43&amp;year=1961">What was here in 1961? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
